    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d512">

                <pb facs="0962" n="838" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg838"/>

                <head>No. 508.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Señor de
                        Zamacona.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-08-02"><hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 2,
                        1881</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your note of the 25th ultimo, in relation to the crossing into Mexico from
                    Tombstone, Ariz. of a sheriff and three persons who captured a fugitive criminal
                    and returned with him to the United States. You consequently point to this as a
                    violation of law and treaty obligation and ask that the guilty parties may be
                    properly punished.</p>
                <p>In reply I have the pleasure to inform you that a copy of your note has been
                    communicated to the Secretary of the Interior, who has been asked to direct that
                    proper measures be taken by the Territortal authorities for the punishment of
                    any guilty parties and for the future maintenance of law and treaty obligations
                    in such matters.</p>
                <p>Accept, sir, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JAMES G. BLAINE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
